    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We conducted a suite of preliminary experiments in which we produced tensile microfractures during dynamic rupture along an interface with frictional and cohesive strength. Ruptures were induced in Homalite samples by exploding a wire embedded adjacent to the interface. During the experiments, the samples were held under an axial load, P, applied at an angle α to the normal to the rupture interface. Test values of α were 30o, 45o, 60o, and 70o and P varied between ~-15MPa and - 30MPa, the latter of which is approximately 85% of the static load under which the samples failed in preliminary strength tests. The dynamic stress fields produced by the exploding wire are recorded using photoelasticity and high-speed digital photography in the laboratory of Ares Rosakis at Caltech.\r\n\r\nRupture velocities during experiments ranged between 50% and 90% of the shear wave speed. Secondary microcracks were produced in the Homalite samples during rupture with a variety of different combinations of α and P. In all cases, the region of shear stress concentration was spread over an endzone (up to ~1cm) behind the traveling crack tip. Initiation and growth of the microcracks appear to be related to these stress concentrations. We observed several interesting behaviors: (i) some tensile microcracks appear to form due to transient stress concentrations of the mode II rupture and appear to be periodic; (ii) some tensile microcracks are associated with rupture termination and develop at the static crack tips; and (iii) a correlation between rupture velocity and microcrack orientation appears to exist. We propose to conduct a series of well-controlled and reproducible experiments and develop a mathematical model of the rupture propagation which explains the observations following the method of Samudrala et al. (2002). Ultimately, the results of this study will be combined with observations of secondary tensile fractures along exposed faults to create diagnostic criteria for interpreting the velocity and directivity of past ruptures.",
